.TH CLOG_CLOSE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_close \- flush, close, and free a logger
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

void clog_close(clog logger);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Releases every resource that the logger owns:
  - When the logger sits on a file and this is the last handle that
    shares that file, this call closes the file descriptor.  While
    another handle (the root or a derived logger) is open, the fd stays
    open and the call lowers the refcount of the file.
    A handle that a second clog_open_file_mp(3) of the same rotating
    file returns shares that file in the same way.
  - When async logging is on (see clog_open_fd_mp(3)) and this is the
    last handle, the call drains everything that is already queued and
    joins the writer thread before it releases the output (and, for a
    logger on a file, closes the file descriptor).  That
    drain is the last delivery attempt of the logger: what it cannot
    deliver has no later retry, so the call writes a "log record
    truncated: undelivered batch dropped (records=<n> bytes=<m>): the
    logger closed" record in its place.
  - When the logger compresses its rotated files (see
    clog_open_file_mp(3)) and this is the last handle, the call waits
    until every compression that a rotation queued has finished, and
    then stops the compression thread.  By the time that the call
    returns, every rotated file of the logger is therefore compressed,
    or left whole and uncompressed after a failed compression.
  - It frees every key=value field that the logger holds.
  - It frees the internal write buffer.
  - It frees the state of this handle, and frees the shared backing
    store only when it releases the last handle.

Do not use the handle after this call.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
logger  Logger handle to close.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Do not call clog_close() while another thread is making any call with
that same handle: a log_* macro, clog_set_level, clog_set_field and
the functions beside it, clog_derive, or another clog_close().  The
caller must make sure that every other use of a handle has stopped
before it closes that handle.  This rule does not cover a close that
runs at the same time as a sibling handle that keeps logging (the root
against a derived logger, or two derived loggers).  Such a close is
safe, because the sibling is a different handle that is open.
For a logger that sits on an fd, from clog_open_fd_mp, this call does
NOT close the file descriptor; the caller must do that.
Asserts when logger is CLOG_INVALID, stale or already closed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_open_fd_mp (3),
.BR clog_open_file_mp (3)
